.TH pridelet 1 "2026-07-29" "pridelet @PACKAGE_VERSION@"
.SH NAME
pridelet \- display large colourful characters with pride flag colours
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pridelet
[
.B \-hkmnpstvVWXLR
] [
.B \-d
.I fontdirectory
]
.PD 0
.IP
.PD
[
.B \-f
.I fontfile
] [
.B \-F
.I filter
] [
.B \-w
.I outputwidth
] [
.B \-m
.I layoutmode
]
.PD 0
.IP
.PD
[
.B \-C
.I controlfile
] [
.B \-I
.I infocode
] [
.B \-E
.I format
] [
.I message
] [
.B \-\-rainbow\fR|\fB\-\-gay\fR|\fB\-\-transgender
]
.RI
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pridelet
prints text using large characters made of smaller characters. It is a fork
of
.B TOIlet
that adds pride flag colour palettes, and it implements features from the
FIGlet specification that TOIlet never implemented: control files,
right-to-left printing, vertical smushing, paragraph mode, layout modes and
word wrapping.
.RI
.SH USAGE
.B pridelet
either reads its input from the command line or from the standard input.
When text is given on the command line, the remaining arguments are joined
with spaces. Otherwise the input is read from the standard input.
.RI
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-font <name>
Use the given font instead of the default value. Fonts are
.B .flf
or
.B .tlf
files stored in the
.B @datarootdir@/figlet
directory. Fonts are looked up first in the font directory, then in the
current directory, then in the system font directory. There is also a
special built-in font called
.I term
that serves as a fallback if no font is available.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-directory <dir>
Specify the directory in which to look for fonts. The default value is set
at build time and usually defaults to
.B @datarootdir@/figlet.
.TP
.B \-s, \-S, \-k, \-W, \-o
Select horizontal character composition rules.
.B \-s
(default behaviour) uses the font's smushing information if any, otherwise
forces overlapping.
.B \-S
forces smushing.
.B \-k
sets kerning (rendering subcharacters as close to each other as possible
without smushing),
.B \-W
renders characters at their full width and
.B \-o
sets overlapping (glyphs slightly overlap the previous one).
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-layout <mode>
Set an explicit layout mode, either by number or by name. This is the
FIGlet-compatible way of selecting how adjacent FIGcharacters are spaced.
The numeric modes are:
.RS
.TP 12
.B \-1
Default (use the font's own layout).
.TP
.B 0
Full width (no spacing reduction).
.TP
.B 1
Kerning.
.TP
.B 2
Smushing.
.TP
.B \-2
Force smushing.
.RE
.RS
The named modes
.B default,
.B full
(or
.BR fullwidth ),
.B kern
(or
.BR kerning ),
.B smush
and
.B overlap
are also accepted.
.RE
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-vertical-smush
Smush successive output lines together vertically. Without this option,
only fonts that enable vertical smushing in their layout parameter use it.
When a font defines vertical smushing rules, they are honoured; otherwise
universal smushing (later sub-characters win) is used. Vertical smushing is
applied to the lines produced when a paragraph or word-wrapped text wraps
to more than one line.
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-width <width>
Set the output width. By default,
.B pridelet
will wrap its output at 80 character columns.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-termwidth
Set the output width to the terminal width.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-paragraph
Put
.B pridelet
into paragraph mode. In this mode, line breaks within a paragraph are
treated as blanks between words, and the text of each paragraph is
reflowed to fit the output width. A blank line separates paragraphs.
This is especially useful when piping a multi-line file through
.BR pridelet .
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-normal
Put
.B pridelet
back into normal mode (the default), in which every newline read produces
a line break.
.TP
.B \-\-word-wrap
Wrap the output at word boundaries so that no line is wider than the
output width. This is implied by
.B \-p.
.TP
.B \-\-justify <mode>
Justify the output horizontally. The mode can be
.BR left ,
.B center
or
.BR right .
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-left-to-right
Print the text left-to-right.
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-right-to-left
Print the text right-to-left. The characters of each output line are laid
out from right to left, as used for scripts such as Hebrew and Arabic.
.TP
.B \-X, \-\-default-direction
Use the print direction stored in the font file. This is the default
behaviour; fonts that do not specify a direction are printed left-to-right.
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-controlfile <file>
Add a control file to the list of control files. Control files
(
.B .flc
files) map input characters onto other characters, similar to the Unix
.B tr
command. The
.B .flc
suffix may be omitted. The file is looked up in the font directory and in
the current directory. See the section CONTROL FILES below for the file
format.
.TP
.B \-N, \-\-nocontrolfiles
Clear the control file list, cancelling the effect of any previous
.B \-C
options.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-filter <filters>
.PD 0
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-filter list
.PD
Specify a list of filters to be applied to the output.
.I <filters>
is a colon-separated list of filters such as
.I crop:rotate:rainbow
and the special argument
.I list
outputs a list of available filters. Several
.B \-F
flags can be specified on the command line, in which case filters are
applied in order of appearance.
.TP
.B \-\-rainbow,
.B \-\-metal, \-\-gay, \-\-transgender, \-\-flag <name>
Shorthand options for commonly used colour effects.
.B \-\-rainbow,
.B \-\-gay
and
.B \-\-transgender
apply the corresponding pride flag palette, and
.B \-\-flag
applies any flag defined in
.BR colors.json .
.B \-\-flag list
lists the available flags, and
.B \-\-metal
adds the metallic filter (same as
.BR \-F\ metal ).
The
.B pride
filter applies the currently selected pride flag palette, or the rainbow
flag if no flag has been selected.
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-export <format>
.PD 0
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-export list
.TP
.B \-\-irc, \-\-html
.PD
Specify the output format. By default,
.B pridelet
will output UTF-8 text using ANSI colour codes suitable for most terminals
such as XTerm or rxvt.
.I <format>
is the name of the export format as recognised by libcaca. The special
argument
.I list
outputs a list of available export formats.
.B \-\-irc
and
.B \-\-html
are shortcuts to commonly used export formats that are guaranteed to
exist.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display a short help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-infocode <code>
Print a
.B FIGlet
infocode. This flag is only here for
.B FIGlet
compatibility, see the
.B figlet
manpage for more information about it.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.RI
.SH CONTROL FILES
Control files are mapping tables used to translate input characters into
font character codes, in the same way as the Unix
.B tr
command. They are typically used to support character sets other than
Latin-1, for example the
.B 8859-2
through
.B 8859-9
files for ISO Latin character sets, the
.B ilhebrew
and
.B ushebrew
files for Hebrew, or the
.B uskata
and
.B utf8
files for katakana and Unicode.
.PP
A control file is a text file whose name ends in
.BR .flc .
Comment lines begin with
.B #
and are ignored, as are blank lines. The optional first line may be the
signature
.BR flc2a .
Command lines have one of the following formats:
.TP 12
.BI t " inchar outchar"
Translate the character
.I inchar
into the character
.I outchar.
.TP
.BI t " inchar1-inchar2 outchar1-outchar2"
Translate every character of the range
.IR inchar1 \- inchar2
into the corresponding character of the range
.IR outchar1 \- outchar2.
Both ranges must have the same size.
.TP
.BI number " number"
A mapping table entry, equivalent to a
.B t
command of the first type. This form is provided for compatibility with
the mapping tables issued by the Unicode Consortium.
.TP
.B f
End the current transformation stage. A control file can contain several
stages: each character is processed by every stage in turn, and within a
stage only the first matching translation is used.
.PP
Characters may be given as Latin-1 characters representing their own
codes, or as numeric codes preceded by a backslash, such as
.B \e65
or
.B \e0x100
(hexadecimal). The backslash escape sequences
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ee ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ev ,
.B \e\e
and
.B \e\ 
(backslash followed by a space) are also understood.
.PP
For example, the following control file converts uppercase ASCII to
lowercase:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B flc2a
.B t A-Z a-z
.fi
.RE
.PP
The commands
.BR b ,
.BR u ,
.BR h ,
.BR j ,
.BR g
and
.BR s ,
which in FIGlet control the encoding of the input bytes, are accepted and
ignored, since
.B pridelet
always decodes its input as UTF-8.
.RI
.SH RIGHT-TO-LEFT PRINTING
By default,
.B pridelet
prints text left-to-right, or according to the direction stored in the
font file. The
.B \-R
option prints each line of output from right to left, so that the first
character of each line appears on the right. This is useful with fonts
that contain characters for right-to-left scripts, such as the
.I ivrit
font. Note that, like FIGlet,
.B pridelet
does not perform bidirectional text layout: with
.B \-R
everything is printed right-to-left.
.RI
.SH VERTICAL SMUSHING
When a paragraph or word-wrapped text is longer than the output width, it
is split over several lines of FIGcharacters. By default these lines are
stacked at their full height. With
.B \-V
(or when the font's layout parameter enables vertical smushing), successive
lines are moved together and overlap according to the vertical smushing
rules defined by the font: equal character smushing, underscore smushing,
hierarchy smushing, horizontal line smushing and vertical line
supersmushing. If the font defines no vertical smushing rules, universal
smushing is used, in which the sub-characters of the later line win.
.RI
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pridelet Hello World
Render "Hello World" using the default font and rainbow colours if
configured.
.TP
.B pridelet --rainbow Hello World
Render "Hello World" with the rainbow flag colours.
.TP
.B pridelet --gay "Love is Love"
Render with the gay men pride flag colours.
.TP
.B pridelet -f big --flag bisexual "Pride"
Render with the
.I big
font and the bisexual flag colours.
.TP
.B printf "This is a long paragraph\enthat will be reflowed.\en\enSecond paragraph.\en" | pridelet -p -V
Render two paragraphs, reflowed to the output width and smushed
vertically.
.TP
.B figlet -f slant -R shalom
Render "shalom" right-to-left.
.TP
.B echo "ABC" | pridelet -R
Render "ABC" so that the first character appears on the right.
.TP
.B pridelet -C 8859-2 -f standard < latin2.txt
Interpret the input using the ISO Latin-2 character set by way of a
control file.
.TP
.B pridelet -m 1 Hello
Render "Hello" using kerning (same as
.BR \-k ).
.TP
.B tail -f /var/log/messages | pridelet -f term --rainbow
Stream the system log through pridelet with the
.I term
font.
.RI
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLORSDIR
Overrides the build-time directory where
.B colors.json
is looked up. The file is also looked up in the current directory.
.RI
.SH FILES
.TP
.I file.flf
FIGlet font file.
.TP
.I file.tlf
TOIlet font file.
.TP
.I file.flc
FIGlet control file.
.TP
.I colors.json
Pride flag colour definitions.
.RI
.SH BUGS
Right-to-left printing does not perform bidirectional text layout.
Vertical smushing is only applied between the lines of a single flush
(that is, within a wrapped paragraph), not between separate input lines.
.RI
.SH AUTHOR
.B pridelet
is a fork of
.B TOIlet
by Sam Hocevar <sam@hocevar.net>. The FIGlet features implemented here
follow the FIGfont specification by John Cowan and Paul Burton. There is a
webpage available at https://signaldirective.github.io .
.SH SEE ALSO
figlet(6), toilet(1)
